WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.100
Understanding logical execution order explains many
SQL behaviors that otherwise feel like

00:00:05.125 --> 00:00:10.075
arbitrary rules The process begins with FROM and
JOIN which determine the

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:16.812
source rows WHERE filters those rows GROUP BY
creates groups HAVING

00:00:16.838 --> 00:00:22.800
filters the groups SELECT then produces the
requested expressions Window

00:00:22.812 --> 00:00:27.638
functions operate after the grouping and HAVING
stage ORDER BY arranges the

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:32.237
final rows and LIMIT can restrict how many are
returned This order

00:00:32.263 --> 00:00:36.825
explains why WHERE cannot use a same-level aggregate
At the WHERE stage

00:00:37.025 --> 00:00:40.925
SUM or COUNT for a group has not been created yet
HAVING

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.850
can test those values because grouping has already
happened It also explains

00:00:45.875 --> 00:00:50.112
why window functions can work with grouped results
They see the rows

00:00:50.138 --> 00:00:54.288
that remain after aggregation and HAVING at that
query level You do

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.462
not need to memorize this list perfectly on day one
Use it

00:00:58.488 --> 00:01:02.588
as a debugging tool When SQL says a column or
aggregate is

00:01:02.612 --> 00:01:06.138
not available where you placed it ask whether that
value exists yet

00:01:06.162 --> 00:01:10.262
in the logical processing order Thinking in stages
is one of the

00:01:10.288 --> 00:01:13.138
best ways to become comfortable with more advanced
SQL
